.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class REGION_COMPLEX>
.br
class csmp::RegionInterface< dim, REGION_COMPLEX >"Policy of the class model for the management of its labeled subdomains that are equivalent to the parts (CAD) or geobodies of the input model\&. 


.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
S\&.K\&. Matthai 

.PP
P\&. Lang 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
2010
.RE
.PP
.SH "Motivation"
.PP
Break the complex interface of the model down into functional units\&.
.SH "Design Intent"
.PP
Policy of the \fBModel\fP that manages any kind of \fBRegion\fP objects, i\&.e\&. \fBModelSubDomain\fP objects that consist of Elements\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
there can be unique (non-overlapping) and non-unique regions\&. A region "Model" that encompasses all elements that form part of the \fBModel\fP class' computational domain is either unique or non-unique, depending on whether there are other unique regions (in this latter case it is non-unique)\&.

.PP
there is a second "fixed" master region that is always there\&. It is always called "Model" and its purpose is as the default computational domain, i\&.e\&. when you compute something on the mdel this region called "Model" will include all parts of the model's computational domain\&. However, the "Model" region does not automatically include the other non-unique regions nor Faces or \fBInterFace\fP objects which are managed by the \fBBoundary\fP and \fBSplitBoundary\fP classes\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Collaborations"
.PP
The \fBRegionInterface\fP relies on the functionality of the \fBRegion\fP derived from the \fBModelSubDomain\fP class\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Interface allows to construct a region or reference regions, for example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// for each distinct value of the 'permeability' a region is created
set<string>  group_names;
model\&.FormRegionsFromPropertyValues( "permeability", group_names );
//    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
cout <<"\\n\\n\\nmain: FormRegionsFromPropertyValues()  creating 'permeability' regions: ";
for ( set<string>::const_iterator it=group_names\&.begin(); it!=group_names\&.end(); it++ ) cout << (*it) <<" ";
cout << endl;
.fi
.PP

.PP
TODO: 
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
!!! all methods that create regions from regions must also copy potential regional property values to these
.RE
.PP

